---
/**
 * Editable homepage article (below the translator).
 * Untouched original: docs/article-source.txt
 */
import AmericanMorseBlock from './AmericanMorseBlock.astro';
import TimingBlock from './TimingBlock.astro';
import { homeFaqs } from '../data/faq';

const link = 'text-accent underline underline-offset-4';
const h2 = 'text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight';
const h3 = 'text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight';
const tableClass = 'w-full border-collapse text-left text-base';
const thClass = 'border-b border-line bg-surface px-3 py-2 font-semibold';
const tdClass = 'border-b border-line px-3 py-2 align-top';
const codeClass = 'font-mono tracking-wide';
---

<div class="mt-12 grid max-w-3xl gap-10 text-[1.0625rem] leading-7">
  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>A Brief Origin of Morse Code</h2>
    <p>
      In the 1830s, Samuel Morse and his colleague Alfred Vail took initiative about Morse code for electric telegraph
      and developed a proper system of dots and dashes in 1837 and finally revealed it publicly in 1838. In 1844, Samuel
      Morse sent the famous message “What hath God wrought?” from Washington, D.C. to Baltimore, over a new telegraph
      line. Without computer technology, people used to send and receive these codes by hand in the form of letters and
      numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Morse code crossed the Atlantic Ocean in 1866 and <a class={link} href="/sos-in-morse-code/">SOS</a> was adopted as
      the international distress signal in 1906. Fun fact: SOS does not stand for any words. It was chosen because{' '}
      <code class={codeClass}>... --- ...</code> is simple and easy to recognize. Morse code was used in emergency
      situations when there was no internet available. It was widely used for telegram and radio signals.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nowadays, you can still find Morse code in ham radio, in aviation beacons, and in tools that help people to
      communicate who have some disabilities. The dots and dashes of Morse code are also used for troubleshooting micro
      electronic systems having just one LED or button. You can spot Morse code in escape room puzzles, movies,
      bracelets and necklaces, and even tattoos.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>About Morse Code Translator</h2>
    <p>
      A Morse Code Translator is an online tool that converts simple text into Morse code — dots and dashes. In Morse
      code, short beeps are known as dots (“dits”) and long beeps are called dashes (“dahs”). You can also convert Morse
      code back into simple English text, which means a message can be coded and decoded with a few easy steps with a
      Morse code translator.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Morse code, there is a unique sequence of dots and dashes for every letter, number and punctuation. Characters
      within a word are separated by a single space while two words are separated by a slash (/). So HELLO is{' '}
      <code class={codeClass}>.... . .-.. .-.. ---</code>, and HELLO WORLD is{' '}
      <code class={codeClass}>.... . .-.. .-.. --- / .-- --- .-. .-.. -..</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      Our Morse code translator works both ways, converting plain text into Morse code and translating Morse code back
      into simple text. This tool can play Morse code out loud with the correct timing. A dash is three times longer than
      a dot, and there are proper pauses between letters and words, so you can easily learn to recognize Morse by
      listening. It all works inside your browser locally, so nothing leaves your device.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>How to Use Our Morse Code Translator</h2>
    <p>It takes only a few seconds. Here is how to convert text to Morse code and Morse code to text.</p>
    <ol class="grid list-decimal gap-3 pl-5">
      <li>
        <p class="font-semibold">Choose your direction.</p>
        <p>
          Choose Text to Morse to convert words into dots and dashes, or Morse to Text to decode a message back into
          English.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="font-semibold">Type your message.</p>
        <p>
          Type your message, or paste your Morse code. Always use a space between letters and a slash (/) between words.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="font-semibold">Read the result.</p>
        <p>
          Your translation shows up instantly. For example, HELLO WORLD becomes:{' '}
          <code class={codeClass}>.... . .-.. .-.. --- / .-- --- .-. .-.. -..</code>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="font-semibold">Play the sound.</p>
        <p>
          Press play to hear your message as short and long beeps. It’s the best way to get used to the rhythm of Morse
          code.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="font-semibold">Copy or swap.</p>
        <p>
          With one click you can copy the result, or swap the input and output to translate it back the other way.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>How to Learn Morse Code Step by Step</h2>
    <p>
      There is no need to memorize a chart to learn Morse code. The best way is to learn it as sound and rhythm, not as a
      picture of dots and dashes.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Step 1: Understand the timing</h3>
    <p>Morse code is built on one simple unit, the length of a dot.</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>A dash is 3 units long.</li>
      <li>The gap between parts of a letter is 1 unit.</li>
      <li>The gap between letters is 3 units.</li>
      <li>The gap between words is 7 units.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Once you learn and memorize this, every message makes sense.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Step 2: Start with two letters</h3>
    <p>
      Begin with E (<code class={codeClass}>.</code>) and T (<code class={codeClass}>-</code>). These two are the shortest
      letters in Morse code, so they explain to you the difference between short and long without any confusion.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Step 3: Add letters slowly</h3>
    <p>Instead of learning the whole alphabet at once, always add a few new letters at a time.</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>A (<code class={codeClass}>.-</code>) and N (<code class={codeClass}>-.</code>) are mirror images of each other.</li>
      <li>I (<code class={codeClass}>..</code>) and M (<code class={codeClass}>--</code>) are easy to hear and remember.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When you start recognizing old letters without thinking, then you can add new letters.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Step 4: Learn by listening</h3>
    <p>
      Before looking at the dots and dashes of each letter in Morse code, always listen to each and every letter by using
      the play button. There is a method called the Koch method; many beginners use this to train their ears to recognize
      whole letters instead of counting beeps. Practicing 10 minutes a day is better than one long session a week.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Step 5: Practice with short words</h3>
    <p>Once you know a few letters, try simple words:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>HI is <code class={codeClass}>.... ..</code></li>
      <li>NO is <code class={codeClass}>-. ---</code></li>
      <li>YES is <code class={codeClass}>-.-- . ...</code></li>
      <li>SOS is <code class={codeClass}>... --- ...</code></li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Step 6: Decode by ear</h3>
    <p>
      Type a short word, press play without looking at the result, and write down what you hear. Then check your answer
      with the translator. Over time, move on to longer words and full sentences.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>How to Encode and Decode Morse Code</h2>
    <p>You can translate Morse code by hand, even without a tool. It works in two simple steps.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Encode: turn words into Morse</h3>
    <p>Pick a short word, like HELLO.</p>
    <p>Write the Morse code for each letter:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>H = <code class={codeClass}>....</code></li>
      <li>E = <code class={codeClass}>.</code></li>
      <li>L = <code class={codeClass}>.-..</code></li>
      <li>L = <code class={codeClass}>.-..</code></li>
      <li>O = <code class={codeClass}>---</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Put one space between letters: <code class={codeClass}>.... . .-.. .-.. ---</code>
    </p>
    <p>If your message has more than one word, put a slash (/) between the words.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Decode: turn Morse back into words</h3>
    <p>Split the message at each space. Every chunk is one letter.</p>
    <p>Match each chunk to its letter.</p>
    <p>Read the letters in order. A slash means a new word begins.</p>
    <p>
      For example, <code class={codeClass}>.... ..</code> splits into <code class={codeClass}>....</code> (H) and{' '}
      <code class={codeClass}>..</code> (I), so the message says HI.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Why Spacing Matters in Morse Code</h2>
    <p>Morse code is easy to misread without spaces. Look at these two examples below:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li><code class={codeClass}>.- -.</code> is AN (the letters A and N).</li>
      <li><code class={codeClass}>.- / -.</code> is A N (two separate words).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When the gaps are changed, even the same dots and dashes meaning get different:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li><code class={codeClass}>.-</code> is the letter A.</li>
      <li><code class={codeClass}>. -</code> (with a space) are the two letters E and T.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That is why every letter needs a space after it, and every word needs a slash. Not every tool uses the same word
      separator, so some use a slash (/), a vertical bar (|), or extra spaces. The main rule is to pick one style and use
      it the same way throughout your message.
    </p>
    <p>Here are a few more examples of letters to practice:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Letter</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse code</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            [
              ['D', '-..'],
              ['G', '--.'],
              ['H', '....'],
              ['K', '-.-'],
              ['R', '.-.'],
              ['U', '..-'],
            ].map(([letter, code]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class={tdClass}>{letter}</td>
                <td class={tdClass}><code class={codeClass}>{code}</code></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Avoid symbols that have no Morse code. Morse code covers letters, numbers, and common punctuation, but not emojis
      or rare symbols. If your message includes them, the translator may show a # in their place. Replace them with plain
      letters or words before you translate.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Try it with a flashlight</h3>
    <p>A flashlight or your phone’s torch is enough to practice.</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>Short flash = dot</li>
      <li>Long flash (about three times longer) = dash</li>
      <li>Short pause between letters, longer pause between words</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Start with SOS (<code class={codeClass}>... --- ...</code>): three short flashes, three long flashes, three short
      flashes. Practice with a friend who is learning too, and never point the light at people’s eyes or at traffic.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Common Morse Code Messages</h2>
    <p>Try typing, tapping, or flashing these:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Message</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Morse code</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            [
              ['OK', '--- -.-'],
              ['LOVE', '.-.. --- ...- .'],
              ['HELP ME', '.... . .-.. .--. / -- .'],
              ['THANKS', '- .... .- -. -.- ...'],
              ['THANK YOU', '- .... .- -. -.- / -.-- --- ..-'],
              ['I LOVE YOU', '.. / .-.. --- ...- . / -.-- --- ..-'],
              ['SOS', '... --- ...'],
              ['GOOD MORNING', '--. --- --- -.. / -- --- .-. -. .. -. --.'],
            ].map(([message, code]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class={tdClass}>{message}</td>
                <td class={tdClass}><code class={codeClass}>{code}</code></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class={h3}>Try These Examples in the Translator</h3>
    <ol class="grid list-decimal gap-3 pl-5">
      <li>
        Encode a phrase. Choose Text to Morse and type GOOD MORNING. You should get:{' '}
        <code class={codeClass}>--. --- --- -.. / -- --- .-. -. .. -. --.</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Decode a word. Switch to Morse to Text and paste this:{' '}
        <code class={codeClass}>-- --- .-. ... .</code> It decodes to MORSE.
      </li>
      <li>
        Break it on purpose. Remove one space from the Morse above and see how the result changes. This shows why every
        letter needs its own gap.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Morse Code Timing Explained: Dots, Dashes & Gaps</h2>
    <TimingBlock />
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>What Does Morse Code Sound Like?</h2>
    <p>Each letter has its own rhythm, a bit like a tiny tune.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={tableClass}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">Letter</th>
            <th class={thClass} scope="col">How it sounds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            [
              ['E', 'dit'],
              ['T', 'dah'],
              ['A', 'di-dah'],
              ['N', 'dah-dit'],
              ['S', 'di-di-dit'],
              ['O', 'dah-dah-dah'],
            ].map(([letter, sound]) => (
              <tr>
                <td class={tdClass}>{letter}</td>
                <td class={tdClass}>{sound}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Put them together and you get SOS: di-di-dit, dah-dah-dah, di-di-dit. It has a short-long-short pattern that is easy
      to remember.
    </p>
    <p>Here is HELLO as sound:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>H: di-di-di-dit</li>
      <li>E: dit</li>
      <li>L: di-dah-di-dit</li>
      <li>L: di-dah-di-dit</li>
      <li>O: dah-dah-dah</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Just type HELLO in the translator, press play and follow along out loud.</p>
    <p>
      Fun fact: Just by timing and rhythm, experienced operators can easily recognize who is sending a message. This is
      called a sender’s “fist”.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Our Morse Code Translator Features</h2>
    <p>
      In our Morse code translator, you can use all features like audio playback, sharing, learning etc. in one place.
      Here is the list below:
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Text to Morse Code</h3>
    <p>
      Type your message into the top box and you’ll see the results in the form of dots ( . ) and dashes ( - ). If the
      result appears in the form of “ # “, it means your character can’t be translated. It offers all letters ( A - Z ),
      numbers ( 0 - 9 ) and punctuation marks.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Morse Code to Text</h3>
    <p>
      When decoding a message, type or paste every dot as ( . ) and dash as ( - ) into the top box. Always put one space
      between letters and put a slash ( / ) between words. You’ll see the result in the form of plain text in the output
      box instantly. It works for Morse that came from a flashing light, a series of beeps, or a note on paper. If you
      find “ # “ in the result box, it means your character can’t be translated; you can spot and fix the mistake easily.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Hear It, See It or Feel It</h3>
    <p>
      Press Play to hear your message as beeps, with short beeps for dots and longer ones for dashes. You can use the
      Pause, Stop, and Repeat button to control it. You can also choose how you want to receive the code:
    </p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>Sound: a clear beep for each dot and dash</li>
      <li>Light: a flashing light that matches the rhythm, like real signal training</li>
      <li>Vibration: your phone buzzes in short and long pulses</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Highlight Feature</h3>
    <p>
      Press Play and watch each letter light up in both the Text and Morse code boxes as it sounds. It is a simple way to
      connect what you hear with what you see.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Customize the Sound</h3>
    <p>You can customize the settings below to make playback fit your level:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>
        Presets: If you are not sure when picking a speed, pick a preset and then fine-tune it with the options below.
      </li>
      <li>Sound type: choose a telegraph click or a CW radio tone</li>
      <li>Pitch (Hz): set the tone to whatever is comfortable for your ears</li>
      <li>Volume: make volume louder or softer</li>
      <li>Speed (WPM): At first, play slowly as a beginner or fast when you are ready</li>
      <li>
        Farnsworth speed: keeps each letter at a normal speed but adds longer pauses between letters; in this way you
        learn to recognize the sound of a letter instead of counting dots
      </li>
      <li>Autoplay: Turn Autoplay button on; it will automatically play your message when it is translated.</li>
      <li>Word separator: You can show or hide the slash between words</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Copy and Share in One Click</h3>
    <p>
      There is also a copy button, so you can grab the text or the Morse code immediately. The share button allows you to
      send your translation straight to people.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Send a Morse Puzzle</h3>
    <p>
      Convert your message into a link and send it to someone as a little puzzle to solve. It is a fun way to challenge
      friends, and it works well for games, parties, and classrooms.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Look Up a Letter</h3>
    <p>If you want to check a letter or a number while translating, just open Reference.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Try a Random Message</h3>
    <p>
      When you are not sure what to type? Press the Random or Example button to load a sample message. It is a quick and
      easy way to practice reading Morse, test your speed, or see how the tool works.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Clear</h3>
    <p>To start a new translation, tap the clear option and both of your boxes will be clear.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Download as Audio</h3>
    <p>
      You can also save your Morse code as a WAV audio file by clicking on the Download WAV button to keep for later or
      share anywhere.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Export TXT</h3>
    <p>If you want to save your Morse code as a text file, then tap on the Export TXT option.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Live Counters</h3>
    <p>
      Below each box, you can see how many characters you typed and how many signals it makes, updated as you type. This
      helps you check message length and see how many dots and dashes a sentence needs.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Simple, Free, and Unlimited</h3>
    <p>
      The design is clean, simple and easy to follow, so you always know what to do next. There is no sign-up, no
      subscription, and no limit on how much you can translate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>When to Use This Morse Code Translator</h2>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-3 pl-5">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Learning Morse by Hearing.</span> Just type a short word, tap play and listen to the
        rhythm. Start slow and build up by using the speed and Farnsworth settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Practice Ham radio.</span> Enter common phrases, set the playback speed to the level
        you want to train at, and listen before you go on the air.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">School projects and science fairs.</span> Translate a message, copy the dots and
        dashes into your report, or save the sound by clicking on Download WAV for your presentation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Names and meaningful words for jewelry or tattoos.</span> Make a name, date, or short
        phrase into dots and dashes for a bracelet or ring. Before finalizing, translate it back to text and play the
        audio to confirm every letter is right.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Puzzles, games and escape rooms.</span> Decode a clue you found, or write your own
        and send it to a friend as a puzzle link.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold"
          >Learning the <a class={link} href="/sos-in-morse-code/">SOS</a> signal.</span
        >{' '}
        Practice the pattern <code class={codeClass}>... --- ...</code> with sound and light so you can recognize it. In
        a real emergency, contact emergency services first, since Morse is not a replacement for calling for help.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Stop Memorizing, Start Spotting Patterns</h2>
    <p>
      You do not need to learn 26 random symbols. Most Morse code letters follow a particular pattern, and once you learn
      and see them, the code gets much easier.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Start with the shortest letters</h3>
    <p>
      E is one dot (<code class={codeClass}>.</code>). T is one dash (<code class={codeClass}>-</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      E is the most common letter in English, so it has the shortest code. The code’s early designer, Alfred Vail, is said
      to have counted letters in a printer’s type cases to decide this.
    </p>
    <p>Learn these two first, because they teach you the difference between short and long.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Use your ears, not just your eyes</h3>
    <p>Try to say the sounds out loud: a dot is “dit” and a dash is “dah”.</p>
    <p>
      Dot-heavy letters like S (<code class={codeClass}>...</code>) and H (<code class={codeClass}>....</code>) sound like
      a fast tapping. Hear one and the other becomes easy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Experienced instructors suggest learning by sound, because counting dots and dashes in your head slows you down
      later.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Learn letters in opposite pairs</h3>
    <p>
      Many letters are exact opposites (dots become dashes and dashes become dots). Try to learn them together; this way
      you can save your time:
    </p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>E <code class={codeClass}>.</code> and T <code class={codeClass}>-</code></li>
      <li>I <code class={codeClass}>..</code> and M <code class={codeClass}>--</code></li>
      <li>A <code class={codeClass}>.-</code> and N <code class={codeClass}>-.</code></li>
      <li>S <code class={codeClass}>...</code> and O <code class={codeClass}>---</code></li>
      <li>U <code class={codeClass}>..-</code> and G <code class={codeClass}>--.</code></li>
      <li>R <code class={codeClass}>.-.</code> and K <code class={codeClass}>-.-</code></li>
      <li>W <code class={codeClass}>.--</code> and D <code class={codeClass}>-..</code></li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Learn numbers with one simple rule</h3>
    <p>
      Every <a class={link} href="/morse-code-numbers/">number</a> is exactly five signals long.
    </p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>
        1 to 5: start with as many dots as the number, then fill the rest with dashes. 1 is{' '}
        <code class={codeClass}>.----</code>, 3 is <code class={codeClass}>...--</code>, and 5 is{' '}
        <code class={codeClass}>.....</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        6 to 9: start with (number minus 5) dashes, then fill the rest with dots. 6 is{' '}
        <code class={codeClass}>-....</code>, 8 is <code class={codeClass}>---..</code>
      </li>
      <li>0 is five dashes: <code class={codeClass}>-----</code></li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class={h3}>Try the decision tree (visual learners)</h3>
    <p>
      Imagine a tree in your mind where a dot takes you left and a dash takes you right. Each step adds one signal, and
      every letter sits at its own spot.
    </p>
    <p>Some learners find this easier to remember than a plain chart.</p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Learn slowly and keep it short</h3>
    <p>Try to add only two or three new letters at a time.</p>
    <p>
      Many learners use the Koch method (full-speed letters, added one at a time) or Farnsworth spacing (fast letters with
      longer pauses between them). Both aim to build sound recognition instead of counting.
    </p>
    <h3 class={h3}>Don’t Skip Punctuation</h3>
    <p>A message with no punctuation feels unfinished. The three you will use most are:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>Period: <code class={codeClass}>.-.-.-</code></li>
      <li>Comma: <code class={codeClass}>--..--</code></li>
      <li>Question mark: <code class={codeClass}>..--..</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Punctuation codes are longer (six signals each), but they follow simple, repeating patterns that are easy to hear
      once you practice. Also useful: send eight dots (<code class={codeClass}>........</code>) to mean “I made a mistake,
      ignore that.”
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Improve Your Morse Code Skills</h2>
    <p>Once you learn the basics, a few habits can help you get better and faster.</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-3 pl-5">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Get it right before you get fast.</span> Start with slow spacing between letters, but
        keep each letter at a normal pace. This way you learn the real sound of every letter, and speed comes naturally.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Practice and work on your weak spots.</span> Always keep a short list of the letters
        or numbers you often get wrong, and practice those first instead of repeating every letter equally.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Try a memory trick, but don’t rely on it.</span> A short phrase or picture can help
        with a tricky letter in the first few days. Some instructors suggest moving on to sound as soon as you can,
        because the trick adds one extra step between hearing and understanding.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Move on to common short codes.</span> After words, learn the short signals radio
        operators use, such as CQ (a call to any station), QTH (my location), and 73 (best regards).
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Make it a game.</span> You can send a Morse puzzle to a friend, decode a random
        message, or race to beat your own time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Learn with others.</span> Ham radio clubs and online learning groups give you
        feedback, practice partners, and a reason to keep going. Most clubs also run Morse contests, where operators try
        to make contacts.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Tap along as you listen. With your finger, drum the rhythm on your desk; if you want a dot tap short and if you want
      a dash press longer. Moving your hand helps the timing stick in your memory.
    </p>
    <p>
      Try this today: Type any message into the translator, press play and write down what you hear before you look at the
      result.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Why Morse Code Is Still Around</h2>
    <p>
      Morse code has lasted more than 180 years because it asks for very little. Just tap on a table; you don’t need a
      screen or keyboard to use it. A flashlight, beep or a tap on the table is enough for you, only if the other person
      knows the code.
    </p>
    <p>
      The origin of Morse code started with telegraph wires, then moved to radio, and in today’s modern era, you can still
      find it in ham radio, aviation beacons and mobile keyboards that help disabled people. With time tools changed, but
      the idea of Morse code remains the same: anyone can learn these simple signals that are sent with careful timing.
      Radio operators call Morse code “CW,” which means continuous wave.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Where You Will Still Meet Morse Code Today</h2>
    <p>
      You can still find Morse code in many places today. Ham radio operators still send Morse as CW, because a simple
      on-off tone can get through weak or noisy signals where a voice may not. Morse code is also used in many aviation
      navigation beacons by repeating their name in Morse for confirmation of a pilot’s right station. Without radio, SOS
      can be sent with a flashlight, a mirror or a tap on metal.
    </p>
    <p>
      People also type with short and long taps by using their mobile, Google’s Gboard keyboard which has Morse input
      mode. Morse code is also used in escape rooms and puzzle games. For beginners it is an easy way to convert messages
      into signals and timings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Tap Input Mode: Send Morse Code with a Tap</h2>
    <p>
      If you want to write Morse code manually, you can switch to the tap input mode. There are three input methods:
      mouse click, tapping a touch screen or the spacebar on a keyboard.
    </p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-1 pl-5">
      <li>Under 200ms tap will register a dot.</li>
      <li>Above 200ms tap will create a dash.</li>
      <li>The letter is auto-decoded after 800ms and appears on the screen.</li>
      <li>Tapping above 2000ms automatically inserts a word space.</li>
      <li>You can also manually click the spacebar button to insert the word break.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Your results are shown visually on screen in the form of dots and dashes in real time. (For example,{' '}
      <code class={codeClass}>-.-</code> appears as you tap it, then turns into K), and every decoded letter is added to
      the output area below the tap pad.
    </p>
    <p>Tap mode is made for learning by doing. You feel the Morse timing instead of just reading about it.</p>
    <p>After a few practice rounds, the 200 ms limit starts to feel natural.</p>
    <p>
      If you already know Morse code then you can key in your message with taps, so there is no need to switch to a text
      keyboard.
    </p>
    <p>The rhythm is close to sending on a straight key, so it is good practice for your timing.</p>
    <p>On mobile, you get a small vibration with every tap.</p>
    <p>Each decoded letter is added to the output box below the tap pad.</p>
    <p>
      From there, you can play your message as sound, flash it as light, or download it as a WAV file.
    </p>
    <p>Copy, share, and download work the same way as in the other modes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Common Mistakes When Translating Morse Code</h2>
    <p>If your result looks wrong, the problem could be in the input. Check these five things first:</p>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Missing spaces between letters:</span> Each letter needs its own space, or the decoder
        reads several letters as one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Missing word separators:</span> Always use a slash (/) between words, or they will run
        together.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Swapped, missing, or extra dots and dashes:</span> One wrong symbol turns a letter into
        a different one. For example, S is <code class={codeClass}>...</code>, but drop one dot and it becomes I (
        <code class={codeClass}>..</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Mixed Morse standards:</span> International Morse and American Morse are different. Old
        telegraph messages may use the older one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="font-semibold">Unsupported characters:</span> There is no Morse code for emojis and rare symbols, so
        you can replace them with plain letters.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cv grid gap-3">
    <h2 class={h2}>Tips to Get More Out of the Translator</h2>
    <ul class="grid list-disc gap-2 pl-5">
      <li>
        During learning, set your speed to 5 (WPM); when you become familiar with letters then increase to 15–20 by just
        dragging the Speed slider.
      </li>
      <li>
        For an authentic CW radio, go to the setting and change Waveform to Square and tone (Hz) to 700.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want to hide input text and just want to read Morse code, just go to the setting and use the Hide Text
        option.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want Morse to text, just type dots and dashes in your Morse code section. In this way you don’t need to
        switch modes.
      </li>
      <li>If you want the experience of light just turn on Flash.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

